`describeStations` returns one summary per input station in input order. Each station requires a non-empty `stationId`, an optional non-empty `label`, and an array containing only finite numeric readings; malformed data throws `TypeError`. A non-empty readings array is summarized using its arithmetic mean and JavaScript's normal number-to-string conversion. An empty readings array uses `N/A`. The exact formats are `Station: [stationId], Label: [label], Mean: [mean]` when a label exists and `Station: [stationId], Mean: [mean]` otherwise. Empty input returns an empty array. Duplicates are retained, and neither the array nor station objects/readings may be mutated.

Known-bad source:
```typescript
export interface StationRecord {
  stationId: string;
  label?: string;
  readings: readonly number[];
}

export function describeStations(stations: readonly StationRecord[]): string[] {
  if (!Array.isArray(stations)) throw new TypeError('Stations must be an array');
  return stations.map((station) => {
    if (station === null || typeof station !== 'object' || typeof station.stationId !== 'string' || station.stationId.trim().length === 0 || !Array.isArray(station.readings)) {
      throw new TypeError('Malformed station');
    }
    if (station.label !== undefined && (typeof station.label !== 'string' || station.label.trim().length === 0)) {
      throw new TypeError('Malformed station label');
    }
    if (station.readings.some((reading) => !Number.isFinite(reading))) {
      throw new TypeError('Malformed reading');
    }
    const mean = station.readings.length === 0
      ? 'N/A'
      : String(station.readings.reduce((sum, reading) => sum + reading, 0) / (station.readings.length + 1));
    const label = station.label === undefined ? '' : `, Label: ${station.label}`;
    return `Station: ${station.stationId}${label}, Mean: ${mean}`;
  });
}
```

